1
00:00:00,690 --> 00:00:04,440
When people decide to move to a new
domain, most of them do the same thing.

2
00:00:04,740 --> 00:00:08,100
They buy the new one,
switch on "forwarding" at the registrar,

3
00:00:08,340 --> 00:00:11,940
type the old address,
and land on the new site. And honestly?

4
00:00:12,240 --> 00:00:13,830
Fair enough. It looks done.

5
00:00:15,325 --> 00:00:18,025
But here's what a half-done move does,
a few weeks later.

6
00:00:18,205 --> 00:00:21,265
Some old links open with a warning:
"Not secure."

7
00:00:21,625 --> 00:00:24,995
Others dump people on the homepage,
not the page they wanted.

8
00:00:25,045 --> 00:00:26,905
Some pages are just… gone.

9
00:00:27,235 --> 00:00:29,845
Google can't follow the move,
so your rankings slip.

10
00:00:30,275 --> 00:00:31,895
And the QR code you printed?

11
00:00:31,985 --> 00:00:34,355
The link in that email from last year?
Dead.

12
00:00:34,936 --> 00:00:37,906
So how do you move your domain the right
way, and keep it secure?

13
00:00:38,266 --> 00:00:40,016
It's simpler than you think.

14
00:00:41,591 --> 00:00:43,841
First, you need to understand three
concepts.

15
00:00:43,901 --> 00:00:45,131
Then we do it together.

16
00:00:46,103 --> 00:00:49,523
Concept one: the 301 redirect.

17
00:00:49,643 --> 00:00:53,453
It's a note that says "moved permanently,"
plus the new address.

18
00:00:53,783 --> 00:00:57,713
Browsers follow it instantly,
and Google updates its records.

19
00:00:58,561 --> 00:01:02,041
Concept two: DNS is the internet's address
book.

20
00:01:02,696 --> 00:01:05,586
Type a domain,
and your browser looks it up in that book

21
00:01:05,636 --> 00:01:08,156
to find a number: the server to talk to.

22
00:01:08,456 --> 00:01:10,226
Those entries are called records.

23
00:01:10,496 --> 00:01:13,616
Moving the right way means changing a
couple of those records,

24
00:01:13,796 --> 00:01:16,946
so the old domain points at something that
hands out the note.

25
00:01:17,482 --> 00:01:20,272
Concept three: the padlock is an ID card.

26
00:01:21,058 --> 00:01:25,408
Over HTTPS, a site shows an ID card first.

27
00:01:25,618 --> 00:01:27,088
It's called a certificate.

28
00:01:27,358 --> 00:01:31,318
It proves the site is who it says it is,
and it keeps the connection private.

29
00:01:31,588 --> 00:01:33,598
No certificate, no padlock.

30
00:01:33,898 --> 00:01:36,958
A warning instead of your page. Here's the
catch.

31
00:01:37,168 --> 00:01:41,648
Your old domain needs its own ID card,
even if all it does is point somewhere

32
00:01:41,698 --> 00:01:45,418
else. The browser checks the ID before it
reads the note.

33
00:01:45,989 --> 00:01:49,809
And browsers are getting stricter:
Chrome is rolling out a warning before it

34
00:01:49,859 --> 00:01:51,989
opens public sites that aren't secure.

35
00:01:52,379 --> 00:01:56,169
To be fair to registrars:
some forwarding handles the ID card for

36
00:01:56,219 --> 00:01:59,249
you. Some doesn't,
and the certificate is on you.

37
00:01:59,429 --> 00:02:01,349
So, some do, some don't.

38
00:02:01,649 --> 00:02:06,119
Here's how to check:
type your old address with https in front.

39
00:02:06,509 --> 00:02:09,539
Padlock? You're fine. Warning? Keep
watching.

40
00:02:09,839 --> 00:02:11,609
That's it: three concepts.

41
00:02:11,729 --> 00:02:15,329
The note, the address book,
the ID card. Now let's use them.

42
00:02:16,418 --> 00:02:19,748
Here's how to do it the right way,
from the RedirHub dashboard.

43
00:02:20,018 --> 00:02:24,198
It handles the note and the ID card for
you, and it works with your DNS,

44
00:02:24,248 --> 00:02:25,388
wherever it lives.

45
00:02:25,876 --> 00:02:28,246
Old domain: mydomain.com.

46
00:02:28,576 --> 00:02:30,916
New site: mybrand.com.

47
00:02:31,460 --> 00:02:33,290
Step one: write the note.

48
00:02:33,530 --> 00:02:36,050
Links → Create → Domain Redirect.

49
00:02:36,465 --> 00:02:38,775
Redirect from: mydomain.com.

50
00:02:39,075 --> 00:02:43,425
Redirect to: HTTPS, mybrand.com.

51
00:02:43,875 --> 00:02:45,445
Type: 301.

52
00:02:45,495 --> 00:02:48,805
And keep the path on,
so every old page lands on its matching

53
00:02:48,855 --> 00:02:51,375
new page. Save. One down.

54
00:02:51,993 --> 00:02:53,893
Step two: the address book.

55
00:02:53,943 --> 00:02:57,273
RedirHub shows you exactly which records
to change.

56
00:02:57,513 --> 00:03:02,073
For a root domain, that's two:
an A record, the number to point at,

57
00:03:02,133 --> 00:03:05,853
and a TXT record,
which proves the domain is yours.

58
00:03:06,123 --> 00:03:11,433
Plus a CNAME for the www version. Quick
tip.

59
00:03:11,733 --> 00:03:15,003
Before you touch anything in your DNS,
screenshot it.

60
00:03:15,123 --> 00:03:17,583
Thirty seconds now, zero regret later.

61
00:03:18,204 --> 00:03:21,064
Now open your DNS,
wherever your domain lives,

62
00:03:21,114 --> 00:03:22,494
and add those records.

63
00:03:22,584 --> 00:03:23,874
Copy and paste.

64
00:03:24,144 --> 00:03:25,974
And copy yours, not mine.

65
00:03:26,274 --> 00:03:29,244
Back in RedirHub,
it checks every few seconds.

66
00:03:29,484 --> 00:03:31,014
The address book updates…

67
00:03:31,164 --> 00:03:33,714
and the ID card is issued automatically.

68
00:03:33,954 --> 00:03:36,154
Two green checks: DNS.

69
00:03:36,204 --> 00:03:37,524
HTTPS.

70
00:03:37,824 --> 00:03:38,974
That's the whole setup.

71
00:03:39,024 --> 00:03:42,784
Concept three took care of itself. Let's
test it.

72
00:03:42,844 --> 00:03:45,694
Open a browser, type mydomain.com…

73
00:03:46,054 --> 00:03:48,184
…and it's still the old site. Hold on.

74
00:03:48,364 --> 00:03:49,924
Your redirect might be fine.

75
00:03:50,194 --> 00:03:51,914
It's your test that's wrong.

76
00:03:53,021 --> 00:03:57,012
Browsers keep copies of pages they've
seen, so you may be looking at yesterday's

77
00:03:57,062 --> 00:04:00,611
site. First rule of testing a move:
a private window.

78
00:04:00,851 --> 00:04:02,681
No copies, no leftovers.

79
00:04:02,951 --> 00:04:06,021
Same address, private window…
and there it is.

80
00:04:06,071 --> 00:04:13,378
mybrand.com. Padlock. Three quick checks.

81
00:04:14,388 --> 00:04:17,538
One: the old domain,
with https in front,

82
00:04:17,598 --> 00:04:19,428
lands on the new site with a padlock.

83
00:04:19,578 --> 00:04:23,598
Two: an old deep link lands on the same
page, not the homepage.

84
00:04:23,868 --> 00:04:27,918
Three: the www version works too. All
three green.

85
00:04:29,133 --> 00:04:30,633
Two things before you go.

86
00:04:31,113 --> 00:04:35,373
A padlock means the connection is private,
not that the site is honest.

87
00:04:35,673 --> 00:04:38,523
It's an ID card,
not a character reference.

88
00:04:38,793 --> 00:04:40,863
And don't let the old domain expire.

89
00:04:41,253 --> 00:04:43,753
If it lapses,
someone else can register it,

90
00:04:43,803 --> 00:04:47,283
and every old link and printed QR code
points at them.

91
00:04:47,613 --> 00:04:51,123
Keep renewing it as long as anyone might
still have the old address.

92
00:04:51,740 --> 00:04:56,600
Remember the start? Forwarding on,
old link opens, looks done. Now it is.

93
00:04:57,981 --> 00:05:01,551
Everything you need to manage your URLs is
linked in the description.

94
00:05:01,821 --> 00:05:05,661
And if you get stuck,
reach out to us. We're happy to help.

